          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line treatment for newly diagnosed high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "According to the hypertension guidance from a major cardiology society (*Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*), four drug classes are first-line for most adults without a separate reason to use something else:\n\n- Thiazide-type diuretics\n- Angiotensin-converting-enzyme (ACE) inhibitors\n- Angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs)\n- Dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers\n\nThe same document says that lifestyle measures come alongside drug treatment. For some people with newly raised readings, lifestyle measures come first, with reassessment after three to six months before medication is considered. Treatment often starts with two drugs when the gap from target is large. An ACE inhibitor and an ARB are not combined, because that adds harm without adding benefit.\n\nThe retrieved text does not explain how a clinician chooses among these classes for a particular person, and the start of the passage was cut off. It also does not say which reading counts as high. Guidance differs by region. The US uses 130/80 mm Hg, while European guidance uses 140/90 mm Hg (from *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*), so the same reading can be labeled differently.\n\nFor a decision about your own care, a doctor or pharmacist can review your readings, health history, and other medications.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
